ple graph once, lazily, each in "},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"order (by vertex index): an isolated vertex, or one whose only edges are loops, is a clique of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one. The order is Eppstein–Löffler–Strash’s: by the cliques’ first vertex in"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"coreNumbers().degeneracyOrdering","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", then as Bron–Kerbosch with Tomita’s pivot (ties to the least","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"index) and branches in ascending index meets them. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"again gives the same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d·n·3^(d\/3)) in all for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"degeneracy d.","type":"text"}],"role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/MaximalCliques","title":"MaximalCliques","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic"},"doc://Cliques/s8SendableP":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/s8SendableP","title":"Swift.Sendable","type":"unresolvable"},"doc://Cliques/St":{"title":"Swift.IteratorProtocol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/St","type":"unresolvable"},"doc://Cliques/s16SendableMetatypeP":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/s16SendableMetatypeP","title":"Swift.SendableMetatype","type":"unresolvable"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/MaximalCliques/Iterator":{"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/maximalcliques\/iterator","title":"MaximalCliques.Iterator","abstract":[],"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/MaximalCliques\/Iterator","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Iterator","kind":"identifier"}],"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind